/-
Fmt/MustBeRefused/M2ProgLT.lean: every proof in this file is false and must be refused. These are
the 19 false proofs of the second toy at tiny parameters, each with the refusal that was expected
of it.
This file holds the bytes of these false proofs written out as Lean literals; it was made by a
program and is not meant to be read.

Tiny parameters (`tinyPar`, Fmt/M2Types.lean) reach no security level. A block is one step of the
protocol. PSC is the sum-check (the head of Fmt/M2Dec.lean).

These files are test fixtures: proofs that every verifier must refuse. Test data: every file here is
false, or breaks a rule of the protocol, and must be refused. A verifier that accepts one of them is
broken. The data are made-up toy data: no model and no weights. Each was made by the toy's own
prover with one block cheating in the named way while all other blocks go on honestly. So each is
expected to pass every test of form, proof of work and Merkle opening, and to fail at the last block
of the proximity test. That each does so is not tested in this file. Fmt/M2EngineLTw.lean tests that
each passes the strict reader and is refused by the decision, the two stages of a verifier that the
head of Fmt/M2EngineLFwa.lean explains; the outcome in Fmt/M2EngineLTw.lean does not tell at which
check the decision refuses.

Sources. This file was generated by a program from files in the archive
toy-files-made-by-the-cuts-tools.tgz (sha256
f97d6774317660e9033841ab36ce4e63e376ebeb25542cf6f1f92e8f61644aa1), which the programs (the C++
implementation that comes with this Lean code) made for the second toy (a chain of three links;
Fmt/M2Dec.lean says what it is). The data are made-up toy data: no model and no weights. Taken from
the archive: FALSE-PROOFS-every-verifier-must-refuse/links/tiny/tiny-links.cheat-X.proof for each
name X, and BOTH-VERIFIERS.tsv in that folder of the archive (the archive is not in this
repository). The generating program stops unless every file that the archive's own list of checksums
(SUMS) names, the lists among them, has the sha256 given there. The file imports no Mathlib. The
false proofs' parameters are those of the honest proof (the parameter bytes are `tiny` in
Fmt/M2Theirs.lean, which holds no proof).

1. What the file contains.
   f_cheat_X        The bytes of the false proof X.
   mustBeRefused    For each false proof: its name, its bytes, and the number of the check at which
                    it was expected to fail.
                      4, the linear check of the last block of the proximity test;
                      5, that block's checks at the drawn positions.
                    That number is taken from the archive's lists. The list of what is expected says
                    of itself that
                      its column of expected refusals was written down before any of these proofs
                        was first made;
                      its column of reasons was mended afterwards for four names, whose first
                        wording a later reading had found too narrow.
                    An earlier archive of the same toy added that nine of the names had been made
                    once before, on an older state of the programs, with the same outcome. Only the
                    numbers are recorded here.
                    Checks 1 to 3: 1, the form; 2, proof of work; 3, a Merkle opening (`table`,
                    Fmt/M2ProgLTFiles.lean).

2. What is checked when the file compiles (#guard). All sizes, and a checksum of each (not sha256,
   which this development does not have). Each byte string is tied to the archive in two steps.
     Step 1, outside Lean. The reference program of the format page (docs/format/FORMAT.md, which
     comes with
       the programs), in the copy that the archive ships (format_rules.py; that copy belongs to
       another edition of the page), computed the format's hash with domain number 168 of the
       file. It did so at the moment when the file's sha256 had been compared with the archive's
       list.
     Step 2, in Lean. A test compares that value with the same hash of the bytes in this file. The
       hash in Lean is `HF` (Fmt/Rules5Fast.lean), which is proved equal to `formatHash` (Fmt/Rules23.lean),
       this library's rendering of the format's hash (`HF_eq`).

3. Theorems. None.

4. What is not claimed.
   No theorem. The tests (#guard) are evaluations that Lean makes when the file compiles. They speak
   of these bytes and of nothing else.
   Beyond what this head reports above of Fmt/M2EngineLTw.lean, nothing is said here about what any
   verifier in this development answers on these files; see Fmt/M2EngineLTw.lean and
   Run/M2Engine*.lean.

Notation: the arguments of `HF`, in order: number, tree, index, start, bytes; `zero32`: 32 zero
bytes; `hex`: bytes as a string of base 16 (both: Fmt/Rules1.lean).

Terms: lean/GLOSSARY.md.
-/
